    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d721">

                <pb facs="0864" n="720" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg720"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.05/183: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Perkins</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-11-27T13:00:00-05:00">November 27, 1929—1
                        p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>389. Your 1005, November 18, 6 p.m. Department believes that in appeals under
                    Article V of Rendition Agreement, Consul is clearly entitled to rights set forth
                    in Article IV of Sino-American Treaty of 1880,<anchor n="13" xml:id="d721-f1"/>
                    including examination of witnesses. In the light of the information given,
                    however, Department believes that it would be preferable to rely on the
                    rendition agreement rather than <pb facs="0865" n="721" xml:id="pg721"/> on the
                    treaty and that present difficulty may best be overcome either by endeavoring to
                    have cases returned to Provisional Court for retrial before a different judge
                    and consular officer or by designating a different consular representative in
                    appeal court.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Cotton</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
